{
  "concept": "A Galilean telescope (convex objective + concave eyepiece separated by f₁ − f₂) takes a parallel bundle entering at angle δᵢ and returns a parallel bundle leaving at the larger angle δₒ = (f₁/f₂)·δᵢ, so angular magnification is just the ratio of focal lengths.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal blue optical axis spanning the full width",
    "Lens 1: tall thin biconvex lens outline (black) on the left, labeled 'lens 1' in blue above it",
    "Lens 2: shorter biconcave lens outline (black) near the right, labeled 'lens 2' in blue above it",
    "Ray A (thick violet): parallel input ray entering above the axis at small angle δᵢ, refracted downward by lens 1, crossing the axis just right of lens 2, exiting below the axis at steeper angle δₒ",
    "Ray B (thin violet): second parallel input ray entering along a lower parallel line at the same angle δᵢ, refracted by lens 1, exiting parallel to ray A after lens 2",
    "Thin blue reference line parallel to the axis at the entry height of each input ray, used to define the angle δᵢ",
    "Curved arrow annotations labeled δᵢ at the left of each input ray (angle between ray and its blue reference line)",
    "Curved arrow annotation labeled δₒ at the right, between the exit ray direction and the optical axis",
    "Dashed violet segment continuing ray B's pre-lens-2 direction to the common focal point",
    "Violet dot marking the common focal point of the two-lens system, below the axis",
    "Small blue dot on the optical axis directly above the focal point, with a short vertical black arrow between them labeled 'd' (the off-axis focal offset)",
    "Horizontal bracket with end ticks under the axis from lens 1 to the focal plane, labeled f₁",
    "Shorter horizontal bracket with end ticks under the axis from lens 2 to the focal plane, labeled f₂",
    "Readout panel showing current f₁, f₂, δᵢ, δₒ and the magnification M = δₒ/δᵢ = f₁/f₂"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "focalLength1",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A longer objective focal length pushes the common focal plane farther right and increases angular magnification proportionally.",
      "label": "Objective focal length f₁",
      "range": [2.0, 8.0, 0.1],
      "default": 6.0
    },
    {
      "id": "focalLength2",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A shorter (stronger) eyepiece focal length steepens the exit rays and raises the magnification f₁/f₂; the eyepiece must move right to stay afocal.",
      "label": "Eyepiece focal length f₂",
      "range": [0.5, 4.0, 0.1],
      "default": 2.0
    },
    {
      "id": "inputAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The output angle scales linearly with the input angle — the ratio δₒ/δᵢ stays fixed while both angles change, and the focal-point offset d = f₁·δᵢ moves off axis.",
      "label": "Input ray angle δᵢ (degrees)",
      "range": [-4.0, 4.0, 0.1],
      "default": 1.5
    },
    {
      "id": "afocalLock",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Afocal operation is a special alignment condition, not an automatic property of two lenses.",
      "label": "Lock eyepiece to afocal position (f₁ − f₂)",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "id": "lens2Offset",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Only when the eyepiece sits exactly one f₂ before the objective's focal plane does the bundle exit parallel; moving it makes the exit beam converge or diverge, so the telescope no longer images at infinity.",
      "label": "Eyepiece shift from afocal position (unlock first)",
      "range": [-1.5, 1.5, 0.05],
      "default": 0.0
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The afocal baseline",
      "narration": "Two parallel rays arrive tilted by δᵢ. The convex objective aims them at a point one f₁ away, and the concave eyepiece — placed one f₂ short of that point — straightens them into a parallel bundle again, now tilted by the larger angle δₒ.",
      "state": { "focalLength1": 6.0, "focalLength2": 2.0, "inputAngle": 1.5, "afocalLock": true, "lens2Offset": 0.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Angle in, angle out",
      "narration": "Sweep the input tilt. The focal point slides along the focal plane (d = f₁·δᵢ) and the exit angle follows in lockstep — but the ratio δₒ/δᵢ never budges.",
      "state": { "focalLength1": 6.0, "focalLength2": 2.0, "inputAngle": -2.5, "afocalLock": true, "lens2Offset": 0.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Magnification is a ratio",
      "narration": "Shorten the eyepiece focal length. The lens separation shrinks to stay afocal, the exit rays fan out much more steeply, and the readout climbs to M = f₁/f₂.",
      "state": { "focalLength1": 7.0, "focalLength2": 1.0, "inputAngle": 1.5, "afocalLock": true, "lens2Offset": 0.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Break the afocal condition",
      "narration": "Unlock and slide the eyepiece away from f₁ − f₂. The exit bundle is no longer parallel — it converges or diverges — so a distant object no longer appears at infinity to the eye.",
      "state": { "focalLength1": 6.0, "focalLength2": 2.0, "inputAngle": 1.5, "afocalLock": false, "lens2Offset": 0.8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Restore alignment",
      "narration": "Return the eyepiece to the afocal spacing and the bundle snaps back to parallel — this is the focusing adjustment on a real Galilean telescope.",
      "state": { "focalLength1": 6.0, "focalLength2": 2.0, "inputAngle": 1.5, "afocalLock": true, "lens2Offset": 0.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both lenses share one focal plane, so the off-axis height d = f₁·δᵢ = f₂·δₒ is the same for each — which forces δₒ/δᵢ = f₁/f₂: the telescope's angular magnification is nothing but the ratio of the two focal lengths.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape SVG. A thin blue horizontal optical axis runs the full width. Far left third: a tall, narrow black biconvex lens outline labeled 'lens 1' in blue above. About three-quarters across: a shorter black biconcave lens outline labeled 'lens 2' in blue. Two violet rays enter from the left travelling slightly downward at identical small angle δᵢ — one thick (upper, entering well above the axis) and one thin (lower, entering just above the axis); each has a faint blue horizontal reference line at its entry height with a small curved arrow and italic δᵢ label at the far left. Both rays kink at lens 1 toward a common point below the axis a short distance to the right of lens 2, kink again at lens 2, and leave as a parallel pair sloping down-right; a curved arrow labeled δₒ sits at the right margin. A short dashed violet segment shows the thin ray's continuation into the focal point, which is drawn as a filled violet dot. Directly above it on the axis is a small blue dot, joined by a short black vertical arrow labeled 'd'. Beneath the axis: a long bracket with end ticks from lens 1 to the focal plane labeled italic f₁, and a shorter bracket from lens 2 to the focal plane labeled italic f₂. Keep the palette: violet rays, blue axis/labels/reference lines, black lens outlines, brackets and 'd' arrow in black. A small corner readout shows f₁, f₂, δᵢ, δₒ and M = δₒ/δᵢ. When the afocal lock is off and the eyepiece is displaced, the exit rays should visibly converge or diverge instead of running parallel, and the f₂ bracket should no longer terminate at the focal plane."
}

